<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  ExportOptions.plist — the options every App Store archive is exported with.

  Use it:  xcodebuild -exportArchive \
             -archivePath  build/BudgetCrab.xcarchive \
             -exportPath   build/export \
             -exportOptionsPlist ExportOptions.plist

  ⚠️ THIS FILE ONLY BINDS THE COMMAND-LINE PATH. Xcode's Organizer ▸ Distribute App
  does NOT read it; it asks the same questions in a dialog, and its
  "Manage Version and Build Number" checkbox is the UI spelling of
  manageAppVersionAndBuildNumber below. It must be UNCHECKED there too.
  GO_LIVE_CHECKLIST §3 carries that as a step, because a plist cannot enforce a
  checkbox.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>

    <key>method</key>
    <string>app-store-connect</string>

    <!--
      THE REASON, because this key is the one that is easy to leave at its default.

      The build number is set by the COMMIT, not by the export. `CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION`
      in project.pbxproj is bumped in a `chore(release):` commit, that commit is what gets
      the annotated tag `v<marketing>-build<n>`, and the store fixture in StoreFixtures/ is
      captured by building that same commit. Three artefacts all assert the same number.

      With manageAppVersionAndBuildNumber TRUE (Xcode's default, and the Organizer
      checkbox's default), Xcode is free to silently increment the build number during
      export when App Store Connect already holds that number. The uploaded binary then
      reports a build the tagged commit never contained — and the tag, the fixture and the
      MANIFEST all keep asserting the old one, with nothing raising a flag. "Which commit
      shipped as 1.0.5 (10)?" stops being answerable from the repo, which is the exact
      question the tag rule in ARCHITECTURE.md exists to keep answerable.

      On the 1.0.5 build 10 upload Xcode was free to do this and happened not to.
      "It did not happen" is not "it cannot happen". FALSE makes the export inert:
      it ships the number the commit says, or it fails the upload and we fix the commit.
    -->
    <key>manageAppVersionAndBuildNumber</key>
    <false/>

    <!-- Signing matches the project: automatic, team 2867U8T596. -->
    <key>signingStyle</key>
    <string>automatic</string>
    <key>teamID</key>
    <string>2867U8T596</string>

    <!-- Upload symbols so App Store Connect can symbolicate field crash reports.
         Relevant while DEFECT_VOICE_INPUT_DEINIT_ABORT.md is open: an unsymbolicated
         AudioToolbox abort in the field is indistinguishable from any other SIGABRT. -->
    <key>uploadSymbols</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Bitcode is dead; state it rather than inheriting a default that may move. -->
    <key>compileBitcode</key>
    <false/>

    <!-- Ship a single universal binary; the App Store thins it per device. -->
    <key>stripSwiftSymbols</key>
    <true/>

    <key>destination</key>
    <string>export</string>

</dict>
</plist>
